ve the covenant back their  bomb  halo 2's biggest change from the get-go  was duel building there was something  extremely novel and honestly badass  about mixing together old and new  weapons  for some entertaining combinations there  was also the addition of a whole new  character to the campaign  the arbiter the arbiter could cloak  himself and remain camouflaged for short  periods of time  but didn't have the flashlight that  master chief's spartan armor suit had to  illuminate dark areas  the health bar from the first game was  no longer a part of the gameplay  instead the player would need to just  worry about the energy shield  new enemies also made their debut in  halo 2 with the brutes  a hostile group that would go on to  fight both the unsc  and the elites  the most significant evolution from  combat of all to halo 2 was the  inclusion of xbox live support  making halo 2 the first game in the  franchise to officially offer online  multiplayer players would simply  select a game mode and then be  automatically connected with other  gamers online  based on the mode and map type bringing  the massive 16 player matches to the  mainstream  rather than just the small subset of  players who had the means to set up lan  parties  halo 2 would also mark the beginning of  a lengthy absence from pc ports for  future titles in the franchise  master chief's story arc in the original  bungie trilogy would reach a  well-deserved climax in halo 3  aided by a nice graphical update with  its debut on the xbox 360.  the campaign expanded to allow up to  four player co-op missions but only two  players could play on each console at a  time  requiring either system link or xbox  live for that full party of four  halo 3 also introduced support weapons  these heavy weapons would take the  player out into a third person viewpoint  and  offer some pretty good firepower at the  expense of mobility  another evolution of its gameplay was  the use of equipment  although you could only use equipment  once each type would shift the gameplay  and allow the player to incorporate  various abilities some of those  abilities included the bubble shield the  trip mine  and the power drain and quickly became  staples in the halo franchise  halo 3 was the first time that players  got to experience forge  allowing players to adjust existing maps  to create an individualized gameplay  experience  one of the dlc maps foundry was one of  the first maps designed specifically  with forage in mind and  all of the maps and forage could be  saved and shared among the community  being on the xbox 360 infrastructure  multiplayer on xbox live experienced  several booms in terms of the user  interface and ease of access  players would group up in parties on the  360 and then hop into matches together  halo 3 took significant steps towards  integrating modes that weren't just  giving different ways to play but  different ways to experience the game  for example theater was a neat concept  that gave players the ability to  re-watch matches and record safe footage  both from campaign and multiplayer modes  and became a popular addition  halo 3 odst's gameplay shifts from the  original trilogy  you play as odsts or orbital dropshop  troopers meaning you no longer have the  ability to dual wield  the energy shield was also gone and in  its place was a mechanic that involved  stamina  creating a gameplay experience similar  to the first halo  odst also offered visual head changes in  its visor mode which would illuminate  enemies allies and items of interest in  all different colors  multiplayer in odst was just halo 3 and  shared halo 3's servers  the multiplayer came on a separate disk  that was practically halo 3 with the  addition of three odst maps citation  heretic and longshore the campaign would  still allow players to go through the  story cooperatively but the structure of  odst's campaign departed from the  typical linear storytelling of past halo  games  odst had a more open world campaign  allowing you to reach several  objectives or beacons in any given order  in the open world section taking place  in new mombasa  you play as a rookie odst but would also  play  as missing comrades during flashback  missions to piece together what happened  to them  this concept of storytelling allowed the  player to gradually unravel the plot at  their own pace  while allowing them to explore a new  mombasa odst  also introduced fire fight which was the  first horde mode in the halo franchise  it consisted of enemy waves growing more  difficult the longer you survived  and the mood also allowed for online  matchmaking system link  local play and split screen co-op  one of the most beloved games in the  franchise halo reach was a prequel to  the original bungie trilogy  the main character in reach was another  spartan super soldier  noble six which meant that the energy  shield was back  but so was the health bar requiring an  extra eye during combat sequences akin  to combat evolved  the rest of the noble team consisted of  a diverse cast of characters which  encouraged different play styles  throughout the campaign  due to the specific gameplay sequences  in one instance you  aggressively pursue an enemy with noble  5 weapons free  another mission would have you posted up  on a sniper outpost with noble 3  requiring more deliberate shots and  movement to make your way past a larger  covenant group  equipment in reach now had a cool down  timer preventing any spamming and moved  away from the single use concept in halo  3.  jetpacks sprint and active camo were  just some of the game's different  equipment allowing for different  gameplay styles and interactions during  combat  for the first time in halo there was an  aerial dogfighting mission  and in this chapter the player flies a  unsc ship and defends the objective by  defeating waves of covenant ships the  gameplay lets you fly from a third  person perspective  gave you several way